---
/**
 * The `page` template — website-generic scaffolding, one repeatable layout. Contact is
 * the first and only document today; About, Colophon, Apologia and Projects follow.
 *
 * ── THE FRAME IS reviews.astro's, MINUS THE RAIL GYMNASTICS ─────────────────
 *
 * Both draw a title, a lead, optional intro copy, a main column and a reserved rail. The
 * difference is that Reviews RENDERS something in its rail, so it needs the transparent
 * <PageHeader> that lets the rail align to the lead rather than the h1, and the zeroed
 * row gap that goes with it.
 *
 * Nothing is rendered in the rail here, so none of that machinery is needed and the
 * default <Grid> produces the board's spacing on its own:
 *
 *     h1 -> lede    16   <PageHeader>'s own margin
 *     lede -> form  64   <Grid>'s row gap, `rhythm-heading-major`
 *
 * Measured against the Contact board (2562:2663): Page Header 48 tall, the frame below it
 * at y=64, and inside that the lede 72 tall with the form at y=136. Which is 16 and 64.
 *
 * ── THE RAIL IS RESERVED BY PLACING NOTHING IN IT ───────────────────────────
 *
 * On desktop a `page` always leaves columns 9-12 standing, with or without content
 * (Andy, 2026-09-04), and the reason is measure: the prose column is `--col-main` (656),
 * which lands near DESIGN.md's 66ch target, while `--col-full` (996) would push it well
 * past. That is the opposite of the Insights index, which spans full width precisely
 * BECAUSE cards have no measure to protect.
 *
 * THERE IS NO EMPTY <aside> HERE, DELIBERATELY. A grid column is reserved by nothing
 * being placed in it — putting content at `--col-main` leaves 9-12 empty by definition.
 * An empty <aside> would be an unnamed `complementary` landmark wrapping nothing, which
 * is worse than no element at all. reviews.astro has a `.rail` only because it has a nav
 * inside it.
 *
 * Below md `--col-main` collapses to `1 / -1` and there is nothing to reserve, which
 * falls out for free.
 *
 * ── BANDS ARE OPT-IN, SO A NULL HERE IS A DECISION RATHER THAN A GAP ────────
 *
 * `pageBands` lists the bands a page carries at all (Andy, 2026-09-04). A band missing
 * from that list resolves to null even when Settings defines a default, which inverts the
 * ladder's usual "the default always wins if nothing else does". So the guard below is
 * not defensive coding — null is the editor having said no.
 *
 * PLACED AS BODY CONTENT inside the page's single band, because that is what the Contact
 * board draws: the form sits in the prose column beside the reserved rail, not in a band
 * of its own.
 *
 * ── AND THAT PLACEMENT IS STILL AN OPEN QUESTION PAST CONTACT ───────────────
 *
 * Contact suppresses the band's own copy (`bandCopy: false`) so its page title and lede
 * introduce the form. A page that does NOT suppress it — About, say, taking the Settings
 * default — gets the form WITH its own "Get In Touch" heading in this same position,
 * under the body copy. Whether that is right, or whether such a page wants it APPENDED as
 * a separate full-bleed band the way RssBand and WorkWithMeBand are, is undecided.
 *
 * `bandCopy` looks like the natural discriminator and is deliberately not used as one:
 * it would couple "does it carry its own copy" to "where does it sit", which are two
 * questions and will not always have the same answer.
 *
 * NOT resolved here, because Contact is the only `page` document and an appended variant
 * would be an unexercised code path shipped on a guess. Revisit when the second page
 * lands.
 */
import {loadQuery} from '../sanity/load-query'
import {PAGE_SLUGS_QUERY, PAGE_QUERY, PAGE_GET_IN_TOUCH_BAND_QUERY} from '../sanity/queries/pages'
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import Band from '../components/Band.astro'
import Grid from '../components/Grid.astro'
import Masthead from '../components/Masthead.astro'
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro'
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro'
import GetInTouchBand from '../components/bands/GetInTouchBand.astro'

export async function getStaticPaths() {
  const rows = await loadQuery(PAGE_SLUGS_QUERY)
  /* The query filters on `defined(slug.current)`, but TypeGen types a projected slug as
     `string | null` because it cannot see the filter — and a null would become the
     literal path `/null`. Narrowing rather than asserting, as insights/[slug] does. */
  return rows
    .filter((row): row is {params: {slug: string}} => Boolean(row.params.slug))
    .map((row) => ({params: row.params}))
}

/* Two queries rather than one projection, and not by preference — folding the band into
   PAGE_QUERY silently untypes it to `any`, with nothing in this file to reveal it. See
   PAGE_GET_IN_TOUCH_BAND_QUERY for the mechanism and the probe that caught it. They are
   independent, so they run concurrently. */
const {slug} = Astro.params
const [page, bands] = await Promise.all([
  loadQuery(PAGE_QUERY, {slug}),
  loadQuery(PAGE_GET_IN_TOUCH_BAND_QUERY, {slug}),
])

/* Loud rather than quiet. Every slug here came out of the dataset a moment ago, so a miss
   means the two queries disagree — which should fail the build rather than ship a page
   with an empty body. */
if (!page) throw new Error(`[[slug]] no page document for slug "${slug}"`)
---

{
  /*
    ── 64 TOP, 96 BOTTOM — THE SAME ASYMMETRY REVIEWS HIT ─────────────────────

    The Contact board's BodyBand is 811 tall with its content frame at y=64 and 651 tall,
    leaving 96 below. `space="band"` supplies the 64 by default and only the end is
    overridden, taking `--rhythm-section` — the token every other 96 in the build uses —
    rather than a literal, so both clamp together at narrow widths.

    Like Reviews, this page has nothing between its content and the footer, which is what
    the extra 32 is for. Do NOT override this from a page rule: `.band[data-space='…']`
    compiles with <Band>'s own scope attribute to (0,3,0) and a page reaching the root
    gets (0,2,0), so it would lose wherever it sat in the source. `spaceEnd` exists for
    exactly this.
  */
}
<BaseLayout title={page.title ?? 'Page'} description={page.description ?? undefined}>
  <Masthead />

  <main>
    <Band as="section" spaceEnd="section">
      <Grid>
        <PageHeader title={page.title ?? ''} lede={page.lede} body={page.bodyText} />

        {
          /*
            `aria-labelledby` is NOT set on anything here. With `bandCopy` false there is
            no heading to point at, and with it true the h2 sits inside the page's own
            main region rather than in a band of its own — so there is no separate region
            to name. RssBand puts the relationship on the <Band> it owns; this component
            owns none, and the naming goes with the frame.
          */
        }
        {
          bands?.touchBand && (
            <GetInTouchBand
              class="page-touch-band"
              message={bands.touchBand.message}
              bandCopy={bands.touchBand.bandCopy}
            />
          )
        }
      </Grid>
    </Band>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /*
   * Both grid items take the main column — 8 of 12, which is the 656 the board draws and
   * the 8-column cap the form keeps on every page, rail or no rail (Andy, 2026-09-04).
   *
   * <PageHeader> is an ordinary block here rather than transparent, so this places its
   * own box; its internal `--page-header-span` rules are inert in that mode. Reaching it
   * at all depends on the component spreading `{...rest}` onto its root, which is how
   * Astro's scope attribute travels from a parent to a child's root.
   *
   * Nothing is placed in columns 9-12, which is how the rail is reserved. See the note
   * at the top.
   */
  .page-header,
  .page-touch-band {
    grid-column: var(--col-main);
  }
</style>
